WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.702
Software agents are starting to act for us.

2
00:00:02.702 --> 00:00:06.422
They ask a bank for a mortgage, share our data, and pay in our name.

3
00:00:06.422 --> 00:00:12.494
This is Answerable: a design for an agent that can be held to account, and a site where you can watch one work.

4
00:00:13.345 --> 00:00:14.576
Start on the home page.

5
00:00:14.576 --> 00:00:18.001
It says what Answerable is for, and it carries this walkthrough.

6
00:00:18.852 --> 00:00:20.418
Then it sets out four goals.

7
00:00:20.418 --> 00:00:21.537
Check what comes in.

8
00:00:21.537 --> 00:00:22.712
Decide what goes out.

9
00:00:22.712 --> 00:00:23.495
Keep a record.

10
00:00:23.495 --> 00:00:26.796
And work with what exists, starting from email and the web.

11
00:00:27.646 --> 00:00:31.056
The site is a way to look inside a working reference agent.

12
00:00:31.056 --> 00:00:34.697
Every run on it is real, and everyone in the runs is fictional.

13
00:00:34.697 --> 00:00:36.430
Below is a guide to each view.

14
00:00:37.282 --> 00:00:39.872
The design is two gates around a black box.

15
00:00:39.872 --> 00:00:43.065
The core, usually a model, can be talked into things.

16
00:00:43.065 --> 00:00:45.354
So it holds no keys and sends nothing.

17
00:00:45.354 --> 00:00:46.378
It only proposes.

18
00:00:47.228 --> 00:00:48.545
Ordinary code decides.

19
00:00:48.545 --> 00:00:52.255
The inbound gate runs five checks on everything that comes in.

20
00:00:52.255 --> 00:00:55.786
The outbound gate runs six on everything the core proposes.

21
00:00:55.786 --> 00:01:01.292
Underneath are four stores: policies, memory, a trust store, and a record of every decision.

22
00:01:02.143 --> 00:01:05.069
What travels between agents is a verifiable message.

23
00:01:05.069 --> 00:01:06.757
The carriage is never trusted.

24
00:01:06.757 --> 00:01:13.903
Everything inside is signed: who sent it, their authority, the content, the evidence, the terms of use, and where it came from.

25
00:01:14.754 --> 00:01:21.521
The specification is a family of eleven layers, each one a question, with interchangeable answers called suites.

26
00:01:21.521 --> 00:01:24.058
One suite per layer makes a configuration.

27
00:01:24.058 --> 00:01:26.898
A suite marked "runs" has a real run behind it.

28
00:01:28.704 --> 00:01:29.641
This is Explore.

29
00:01:29.641 --> 00:01:32.394
It replays a recorded run, one check at a time.

30
00:01:32.394 --> 00:01:35.030
In the middle are the agents and their gates.

31
00:01:35.030 --> 00:01:37.783
On the right is the detail of the current step.

32
00:01:37.783 --> 00:01:41.239
Along the top is the configuration, one selector per layer.

33
00:01:41.239 --> 00:01:43.464
And the badge says this is a real run.

34
00:01:44.315 --> 00:01:47.024
On the left is Sam's phone: what the person sees.

35
00:01:47.024 --> 00:01:54.544
Sam has signed a mandate, and this screen lists what the agent may do on its own, what it must ask about first, and the spending limits.

36
00:01:54.544 --> 00:01:57.419
Then Sam asks for a mortgage in principle on a flat.

37
00:01:58.269 --> 00:02:00.808
The core proposes searching a product index.

38
00:02:00.808 --> 00:02:05.021
Before anything goes out, the outbound gate asks who is on the other end.

39
00:02:05.021 --> 00:02:11.541
It finds the index on Sam's own list, accepted for product search only, and the lookup is kept with the decision.

40
00:02:12.399 --> 00:02:16.058
To show the deposit, the agent asks Sam's bank for a statement.

41
00:02:16.058 --> 00:02:17.510
Now watch the other side.

42
00:02:17.510 --> 00:02:23.085
The bank's inbound gate checks the authority, and for a balance it wants a certified face check.

43
00:02:23.085 --> 00:02:25.815
The request is held, and a challenge goes back.

44
00:02:26.665 --> 00:02:31.206
Sam's phone shows the request: confirm it is you, who is asking, and what for.

45
00:02:31.206 --> 00:02:32.719
Sam approves with Face ID.

46
00:02:32.719 --> 00:02:38.425
That screen is labelled illustrative, because the run records Sam's signed answer, not the prompt.

47
00:02:39.276 --> 00:02:43.704
The lender asks for evidence, and the core proposes sharing two statements.

48
00:02:43.704 --> 00:02:45.889
The outbound gate goes down its list.

49
00:02:45.889 --> 00:02:47.188
The mandate covers it.

50
00:02:47.188 --> 00:02:48.723
The recipient is accepted.

51
00:02:48.723 --> 00:02:50.199
Only what is needed goes.

52
00:02:50.199 --> 00:02:52.029
The terms on the data allow it.

53
00:02:52.029 --> 00:02:55.572
Then limits and approval: the mandate says to ask Sam first.

54
00:02:56.422 --> 00:03:00.248
The approval sheet says what is shared, with whom, and on what terms.

55
00:03:00.248 --> 00:03:06.070
Sam approves, the answer is signed on the phone for this exact action, and only then does the message go.

56
00:03:06.922 --> 00:03:08.247
Here is that message.

57
00:03:08.247 --> 00:03:12.982
At every gate the data is RDF: linked data, with its meaning made explicit.

58
00:03:12.982 --> 00:03:17.590
The inspector shows the packet as JSON-LD, or, with one click, as Turtle.

59
00:03:17.590 --> 00:03:20.746
Below it, each layer of the message can be opened.

60
00:03:21.734 --> 00:03:24.815
Next the agent arranges a valuation, which has a fee.

61
00:03:24.815 --> 00:03:26.734
Payment is treated as a contract.

62
00:03:26.734 --> 00:03:28.362
First the service is agreed.

63
00:03:28.362 --> 00:03:34.118
Sam approves the valuer's offer of four hundred and fifty dollars, inside the limit in the mandate.

64
00:03:34.968 --> 00:03:43.133
Then the invoice comes in, and the gate checks the payment against that agreement: the same amount, the same payee, within the mandate's limits.

65
00:03:43.133 --> 00:03:45.911
Sam approves the exact order, again with Face ID.

66
00:03:45.911 --> 00:03:48.576
Then the gate builds the payment order, in RDF.

67
00:03:49.426 --> 00:03:54.192
At the point of payment, the stage carries a selector: how the payment is settled.

68
00:03:54.192 --> 00:03:56.110
The baseline describes it in RDF.

69
00:03:56.110 --> 00:04:00.877
Choose another payment protocol, and the explorer loads the real run that used it.

70
00:04:00.877 --> 00:04:03.202
The same checks run whichever is chosen.

71
00:04:04.053 --> 00:04:05.696
Every selector works this way.

72
00:04:05.696 --> 00:04:11.227
Change the transport from a web call to email, and you are watching the run that was made over email.

73
00:04:11.227 --> 00:04:15.717
Each option you can pick is a real run of the engine, and the badge still says so.

74
00:04:16.569 --> 00:04:19.377
The design matters most when something goes wrong.

75
00:04:19.377 --> 00:04:25.218
In this run the core is fooled by text in a message, and proposes paying four hundred and fifty dollars.

76
00:04:25.218 --> 00:04:29.598
The outbound gate finds nothing in Sam's mandate that allows it, and stops it.

77
00:04:29.598 --> 00:04:30.441
Nothing leaves.

78
00:04:31.291 --> 00:04:34.562
And here the lender asks for more than the mandate covers.

79
00:04:34.562 --> 00:04:35.239
Same result.

80
00:04:35.239 --> 00:04:38.227
The gate stops it, and the agent tells the lender no.

81
00:04:39.079 --> 00:04:40.635
The mortgage is one example.

82
00:04:40.635 --> 00:04:48.247
The catalogue holds seventy situations in fifteen sectors, and says plainly where the design fits, where it strains, and where it breaks.

83
00:04:49.096 --> 00:04:54.188
Each has a main path, and the things that go wrong: two hundred and eighty-three paths in all.

84
00:04:54.188 --> 00:04:59.821
Two hundred and seventy-eight are real runs, and each gave the outcome that was written down beforehand.

85
00:04:59.821 --> 00:05:00.905
Five cannot run yet.

86
00:05:00.905 --> 00:05:04.696
They wait on a proof package, and each is listed with what is missing.

87
00:05:05.547 --> 00:05:07.773
Every check traces back to a requirement.

88
00:05:07.773 --> 00:05:12.171
This view takes ten published risks for agents, and shows the answer to each one.

89
00:05:14.434 --> 00:05:16.105
The specifications are here too.

90
00:05:16.105 --> 00:05:21.538
They are drafts, with every requirement numbered, so a step in a run can point to the clause it applied.

91
00:05:22.485 --> 00:05:25.711
Most people will not adopt a new protocol in one step.

92
00:05:25.711 --> 00:05:29.535
So an agent's email carries a short footer that says what it is.

93
00:05:29.535 --> 00:05:31.626
Someone with no agent just replies.

94
00:05:31.626 --> 00:05:33.717
Someone with an agent can check it.

95
00:05:34.566 --> 00:05:35.627
That is Answerable.

96
00:05:35.627 --> 00:05:41.157
To be plain about it: both sides of every run use the same code, and the specifications are drafts.

97
00:05:41.157 --> 00:05:42.553
Every view can be linked.

98
00:05:42.553 --> 00:05:43.950
Step through it yourself.
